# Line endings are a property of this REPO, not of whatever core.autocrlf each clone happens
# to set. The history has always been all-LF, but only because every clone so far was
# configured that way by hand - a machine setting, invisible to CI and to anyone cloning.
# `text=auto eol=lf` moves the guarantee into the repo: LF in every blob, LF in every working
# tree, on every platform. It was a verified no-op on content when added (0 of 1192 tracked
# non-binary blobs held a CR), so it ratified the existing history rather than rewriting it.
#
# The WORKING TREE matters as much as the history because the code here is written largely by
# tools, which read and rewrite exact bytes. A CRLF working copy turns every multi-line edit
# into an ending-matching problem, and every whole-file rewrite into a silent flip to LF that
# produces no diff at all (staging normalizes it away). That drift was already measurable -
# 323 of 1056 tracked text files had flipped to LF on disk before this rule landed. A uniform
# tree removes the whole class, and removes the need for an AGENTS.md rule about it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Source is text BY DEFINITION, never by content sniffing. `text=auto` decides by scanning for
# a NUL byte, so a source file that embeds one is silently reclassified as binary and skipped
# by normalization. Declaring the source extensions `text` takes that guess out of the loop.
#
# This does NOT make such a file diffable or searchable, and it is important not to read it
# that way: `text` governs normalization only. Diffing consults the separate `diff` attribute,
# and grep/ripgrep do their own sniffing that no git attribute can reach - a NUL-bearing
# source file prints "Binary file matches" with no line, which makes it invisible to code
# search and therefore to any agent working in this repo. The only real guard is to not embed
# NUL bytes in source at all; src/editor/lineEndings.test.ts asserts that.
*.ts text
*.tsx text
*.mjs text
*.cjs text
*.js text

# --- exceptions: the files that break with the wrong line ending ----------------------------

# cmd.exe parses labels and parenthesised blocks byte-wise. launch.cmd has a `goto ready`
# inside a `for /l %%i in (1,1,90) do ( )` block - the exact construct that dies with "The
# system cannot find the batch label specified" if the file arrives with LF. launch.vbs is
# pinned beside it; both are Windows-only by definition. src/editor/lineEndings.test.ts
# asserts they stay CRLF, because a tool editing them would otherwise write LF silently and
# break the desktop launcher without failing a single test.
apps/desktop/launch.cmd text eol=crlf
apps/desktop/launch.vbs text eol=crlf

# Redundant under the blanket rule above, kept deliberately: this is the one file where LF is
# required rather than stylistic. It is executed by a Linux shell straight out of a
# WINDOWS working tree, and bash does not tolerate CR (`$'\r': command not found`). If the
# blanket rule is ever relaxed, this line is what keeps the WSL oracle working. server/*.sh
# are checked out on the server, where autocrlf is off, so they need no rule.
scripts/oracle-wsl.sh text eol=lf
